// Original chapter guides. Titles/page spans verified in the supplied editions.
// Model links are supporting foundations, not claims of complete textbook coverage.
const chapter=(course,number,title,pages,overview,ideas,misconception,questions,topicIds=[],modelNote='')=>({
  id:`${course}-ch${number}`,courseId:course,number,title,pages,overview,
  ideas:ideas.map(([title,explanation])=>({title,explanation})),
  misconceptions:[{claim:misconception[0],correction:misconception[1]}],
  questions:questions.map(([prompt,answer])=>({prompt,answer})),topicIds,modelNote
});
const ward=[
  chapter('108',1,'Introducing cognitive neuroscience',[1,18],
    'Connect a cognitive explanation of a task with a biological explanation of its implementation. Useful theories make predictions about both behavior and neural evidence; naming an active brain region alone does not explain the computation.',
    [['Levels of explanation','Separate the task being solved, the representations and operations used, and their neural implementation. Evidence at one level constrains the others.'],['Specialization within networks','Regions can make different contributions while interacting. A cognitive function need not belong to one isolated anatomical location.'],['Converging evidence','Compare behavioral experiments, recording, imaging, lesion and stimulation evidence. Their different limitations make agreement more informative.']],
    ['An activation map explains a mental process.','Localization supplies evidence about implementation. A mechanistic account also specifies the information represented, transformations performed and predictions that distinguish it from alternatives.'],
    [['A region activates during two tasks. Does this prove the tasks use the same process?','No. Shared activation can reflect several computations, common demands or mixed neuronal populations. Compare task manipulations and independent behavioral or causal evidence.'],['How can a neural finding improve a cognitive model?','It can constrain timing, connectivity or plausible representations, and motivate a behavioral prediction. The model must still explain performance rather than only fit a map.']],['brain-overview','cerebral-cortex'],'Anatomical orientation supports the chapter; these models do not depict a cognitive theory.'),
  chapter('108',2,'Introducing the brain',[19,34],
    'Build an anatomical frame from neuron to hemisphere. Distinguish soma, dendrites, axon and synapse; then place cortical lobes, white matter and deep structures in standard views. Keep anatomical position separate from a claim about function.',
    [['Cellular communication','Action potentials propagate along an axon; chemical synapses change postsynaptic conductance. Effects depend on receptors, gradients and circuit context.'],['Gray and white matter','Gray matter contains somata, dendrites, synapses and glia. White matter contains axons and supporting cells; it is more than an inert cable.'],['Anatomical organization','Use medial/lateral, anterior/posterior and superior/inferior, plus coronal, sagittal and axial planes, to locate cortical and subcortical structures.']],
    ['A structure has one function, and its color identifies it.','Models use conventional teaching colors. Identify shape, neighboring structures and orientation; a named region can participate in several interacting systems.'],
    [['Follow a signal from a dendritic input to a terminal bouton. What changes at the synapse?','Membrane currents integrate across the receiving neuron; an axonal spike can trigger calcium-dependent vesicle release. Transmitter binding changes conductance in the next cell rather than continuing the same spike.'],['Why is a hippocampal outline without a surrounding brain hard to identify?','Orientation, its medial temporal position, curvature and relationship to neighboring structures provide recognition cues. An isolated outline can hide those constraints.']],
    ['brain-overview','cerebral-cortex','thalamus','hypothalamus','hippocampus','amygdala','cerebellum','brainstem','white-matter','corpus-callosum','association-fibers','neuron','dendrites','myelin','resting-potential','synaptic-release'],
    'Core anatomical foundations. Fine molecular and cellular details in the models go beyond this introductory chapter.'),
  chapter('108',3,'The electrophysiological brain',[35,54],
    'Ask what an electrical measurement samples and when it can distinguish processes. Single-unit spikes, scalp EEG, averaged ERPs and MEG provide different views of neural activity. Good temporal resolution does not automatically provide a unique source location.',
    [['Single-unit recordings','Relate spike responses to controlled stimulus or task changes. A selective response supports a coding hypothesis without proving one neuron is the entire representation.'],['EEG and ERP','Scalp signals reflect summed fields from populations. An ERP averages activity time-locked to an event; trial variation and overlapping components still matter.'],['Timing versus location','Compare latency, amplitude and topography with explicit task contrasts. Source inference requires assumptions because several internal sources can produce similar external fields.']],
    ['EEG directly reads individual action potentials at the scalp.','Scalp EEG mainly reflects synchronized population currents, especially postsynaptic currents in suitably aligned neurons. It is not a single-neuron spike counter.'],
    [['A manipulation changes ERP amplitude but not latency. What follows?','It changes the measured event-related response, but amplitude alone does not uniquely reveal a specific process or source. Check component overlap, task demands and alternative explanations.'],['Why combine EEG with fMRI?','EEG offers fine temporal information; fMRI offers complementary spatial and hemodynamic information. Agreement can constrain an account, while the methods still measure different biological signals.']],['neuron','dendrites','resting-potential','synaptic-integration'],'Cellular models explain signal origins. No EEG/ERP/MEG dataset is simulated here.'),
  chapter('108',4,'The imaged brain',[55,86],
    'Distinguish anatomical images from functional contrasts. Interpret an activation as a measured difference between conditions, then ask whether the experiment isolates the process of interest. Brain images require the same inference discipline as behavioral data.',
    [['Structure versus function','Structural scans describe tissue organization; functional methods track physiological changes associated with activity. Diffusion-based tractography estimates pathways rather than observing individual axons.'],['BOLD and experimental contrasts','fMRI BOLD reflects blood-oxygenation changes coupled to neural activity. A contrast depends on its baseline, timing and the demands that differ between conditions.'],['Analysis and inference','Registration, smoothing, statistics and multiple-comparison control affect the result. Predictive decoding and identifying a necessary mechanism are different achievements.']],
    ['A bright fMRI area is necessary for the task or reveals the participant’s thought.','A contrast is correlational. Necessity needs converging causal evidence, and reverse inference depends on how specifically the activity is associated with the proposed process.'],
    [['Task A differs from baseline in difficulty as well as memory. What is the interpretive problem?','The contrast may reflect difficulty, control, motor responses or other differences, not memory alone. Improve controls or use factorial/parametric designs to test these alternatives.'],['What does an MRI-derived streamline represent?','An estimated path supported by diffusion measurements and tracking assumptions. It is not a directly imaged axon, a guaranteed synaptic connection or a direction-of-signal measurement.']],['brain-overview','white-matter','myelin','astrocytes'],'Atlas/tract context and the neurovascular interface are supporting models; they are not the chapter’s imaging experiments.'),
  chapter('108',5,'The lesioned brain and stimulated brain',[87,114],
    'Use disruption to test whether a region or component contributes to a task. Lesions and stimulation can strengthen causal inference, but neither selectively removes one abstract mental operation. Interpret the manipulation and behavior together.',
    [['Dissociation','A single dissociation can reflect unequal task difficulty. Opposing patterns across cases strengthen evidence that tasks depend on partly different resources.'],['Lesion inference','Consider lesion extent, connectivity, pre-existing differences, compensation and recovery. A deficit can reflect damage to a network or pathway as well as a local region.'],['Stimulation controls','TMS and electrical stimulation differ in mechanisms and resolution. Sham, control sites, timing and nonspecific sensory effects help interpret a result.']],
    ['A double dissociation proves two perfectly independent modules.','It supports separable contributions under the tested conditions. Unequal task demands, distributed networks and alternative architectures still require consideration.'],
    [['Patient A struggles with faces but not objects; B shows the reverse. What is supported?','The tasks depend on at least partly distinguishable resources. Establish comparable tests and reliable effects before claiming a particular neural or computational architecture.'],['Why is a control stimulation site useful?','It helps separate a location-specific effect from sound, discomfort, expectation or general distraction. It complements rather than replaces other controls.']],['brain-overview','cerebral-cortex','corpus-callosum'],'Anatomy and disconnection context only; no lesion patient or stimulation field is modeled.'),
  chapter('108',6,'The developing brain',[115,142],
    'Development changes connections, myelination and functional organization across time. Genes and environments interact through development; neither is a complete blueprint. Distinguish a population statistic from a causal explanation of one individual.',
    [['Changing structure','Synapses, dendrites, axons and myelin have different developmental trajectories. Structural change does not translate into one simple measure of maturity.'],['Sensitive periods','Experience can have age-dependent effects. A sensitive period implies heightened responsiveness, not that all learning becomes impossible afterward.'],['Heritability in context','Heritability describes variation within a studied population and environment. Gene–environment correlation and interaction complicate causal interpretation.']],
    ['High heritability means an ability cannot change.','A variance estimate does not specify immutability or the percentage of an individual caused by genes. Environmental interventions can matter even for a heritable trait.'],
    [['Why should a cross-sectional age difference be interpreted cautiously?','Groups can differ in cohort, education, experience and sampling as well as age. Longitudinal and converging evidence better constrain developmental trajectories.'],['How can genes and experience jointly influence development?','Genes affect cellular processes and responses to experience; environments influence expression and opportunities for learning. Their contributions need not add independently.']],['cerebral-cortex','white-matter','myelin','structural-plasticity','homeostatic-plasticity'],'These are biological foundations, not measurements of a developmental trajectory.'),
  chapter('108',7,'The seeing brain',[143,174],
    'Trace visual information from eye through subcortical relays into cortex, then distinguish the computations needed for perception, recognition and visually guided action. Compare neural selectivity with lesion, behavioral and imagery evidence.',
    [['Visual pathway','For the principal cortical route, retinal signals travel through optic pathways to LGN, then through optic radiation to visual cortex. Keep visual field and eye of origin distinct.'],['Specialized interacting processing','Visual areas analyze features and objects through interacting pathways. Ventral recognition and dorsal action distinctions are useful without implying complete independence.'],['Awareness and residual vision','Blindsight separates some visually guided performance from reported awareness. Object and face recognition deficits constrain different stages and representations.']],
    ['The left eye sends all of its information to the right brain.','Each hemisphere receives information primarily about the opposite visual field from both eyes. The optic chiasm crossing pattern is not an entire-eye swap.'],
    [['How does optic radiation differ from the optic tract?','The optic tract is upstream of LGN; optic radiation carries the post-LGN thalamocortical projection toward visual cortex. Radiation fibers do not cross at the optic chiasm.'],['Why is successful forced-choice performance not identical to conscious seeing?','Performance and reported experience are different measurements. Residual discrimination can occur despite absent or reduced reported visual awareness.']],['optic-radiation','thalamus','cerebral-cortex','brainstem'],'Models orient visual anatomy. Retina, feature maps and recognition tasks are covered by the notes rather than a new 3D scene.'),
  chapter('108',8,'The hearing brain',[175,202],
    'Connect physical sound properties with auditory processing, then separate recognizing speech, voices and music. A common sensory input can support several computations; behavioral contrasts and neural evidence help identify their relationships.',
    [['Sound and coding','Frequency, intensity and temporal structure contribute to hearing. Tonotopic organization is a mapping principle, not a complete account of perceived pitch or meaning.'],['Auditory pathways','Signals pass through multiple brainstem, thalamic and cortical stages, with extensive bilateral projections and interactions.'],['Complex auditory recognition','Speech perception combines acoustic cues with learned categories and context. Music and voice perception have overlapping and distinguishable demands.']],
    ['One temporal-lobe location performs all hearing.','Auditory processing involves multiple stages and interacting networks. A response to speech can reflect acoustics, phonology, recognition or other task demands.'],
    [['How would you test a claimed speech-specific response?','Compare speech with carefully chosen nonspeech controls that match relevant acoustic and task properties. Then vary intelligibility or language structure to separate possible causes.'],['Why is a tone-frequency map not a map of word meanings?','Tonotopy concerns sound frequency. Word meaning requires additional learned representations and interactions beyond the physical-frequency organization.']],['brainstem','thalamus','cerebral-cortex'],'Anatomical background only; the current atlas does not delineate the full auditory pathway or tonotopic maps.'),
  chapter('108',9,'The attending brain',[203,232],
    'Distinguish selecting a location, feature or object from sustaining a goal and orienting to an unexpected event. Attention changes processing and performance through distributed mechanisms. Neglect illustrates that awareness and sensory input can come apart.',
    [['Selection and behavioral cost','Use valid, invalid and neutral cues to separate benefits from costs. Eye position, awareness and attention are related but distinct.'],['Frontoparietal interactions','Goal-driven and stimulus-driven orienting recruit interacting systems. The relevant network depends on the task and how the contrast is constructed.'],['Neglect and competition','Neglect can affect represented space or objects and need not be explained by primary sensory loss. Competing stimuli can expose deficits absent for isolated items.']],
    ['Failing to report an item proves the eyes or visual cortex did not register it.','Selection, awareness, task demands and response processes can affect reporting after sensory input is available. Test alternative explanations rather than treating omission as blindness.'],
    [['What does an invalid spatial cue add to an attention experiment?','It tests the cost of orienting or preparing for the wrong location. Neutral trials help distinguish that cost from a valid-cue benefit.'],['How can extinction differ from loss of sight?','A contralesional item may be detected alone but missed when an ipsilesional competitor is present. This implicates selection/competition rather than simple absence of visual input.']],['cerebral-cortex','thalamus','association-fibers'],'Supporting network anatomy; attention and neglect experiments are explained in the notes.'),
  chapter('108',10,'The acting brain',[233,264],
    'Organize movement from selecting a goal through preparing, executing and monitoring an action. Distinguish cortical, basal-ganglia and cerebellar loops from descending motor pathways. Behavior tests what those circuits contribute, not just where they are.',
    [['Cortical motor organization','Primary motor, premotor and supplementary motor territories make distinguishable contributions within connected systems. Preparation is not the same measurement as muscle execution.'],['Loops versus descending output','Basal-ganglia and cerebellar loops influence cortical action systems through relays. The corticospinal pathway is a distinct descending projection.'],['Action perception and awareness','Ownership, agency, imitation and object-directed action can dissociate. Neural responses during observation do not alone establish an explanation of understanding.']],
    ['A circuit loop is one unbroken axon from cortex to muscle.','Loops include synapses, relays and cell populations. A tract visualization and a signed circuit diagram represent different biological relationships.'],
    [['What distinguishes cerebellar and basal-ganglia contributions from corticospinal output?','They participate in recurrent selection, coordination and learning systems. Corticospinal axons form a descending route toward spinal motor circuitry rather than those recurrent loops.'],['Does activation while observing an action prove a neuron understands the actor’s intention?','No. It supports a relation to observed-action processing, but competing sensory, motor or associative explanations need tests. Behavioral and causal evidence help constrain the claim.']],['cerebral-cortex','corticospinal','dorsal-column','thalamus','basal-ganglia','cerebellum','cerebellar-circuit','dopamine'],'Movement pathways and signed circuit foundations; instructional timing is not recorded motor behavior.'),
  chapter('108',11,'The remembering brain',[265,298],
    'Compare working memory, episodic remembering, familiarity, skills and other forms of long-term memory. Amnesia and carefully designed tasks reveal different contributions of medial temporal and prefrontal systems. Keep systems-level memory separate from synaptic change.',
    [['Memory is not one capacity','Different tests demand maintenance, manipulation, recognition, recollection or skill expression. A deficit on one task need not generalize to all memory.'],['Medial temporal organization','Hippocampus interacts with entorhinal, perirhinal and parahippocampal territories. Gross hippocampal anatomy and CA/DG circuitry describe different scales.'],['Encoding and retrieval control','Prefrontal contributions include organizing encoding and guiding retrieval. Consolidation and the continuing role of hippocampus depend on memory type and theory.']],
    ['Long-term potentiation is itself an autobiographical memory.','LTP is a form of synaptic change. Remembering an event requires representations and coordinated activity across systems; cellular plasticity alone does not specify its content.'],
    [['Why can someone with amnesia improve a skill without recalling the practice sessions?','Skill learning and conscious episodic recall can depend on different resources. The test must distinguish improved performance from awareness of the learning episode.'],['What evidence would separate recollection from familiarity?','Use tasks or judgments that distinguish contextual details from a sense of prior occurrence, while considering difficulty and response biases. Compare behavioral patterns with converging neural evidence.']],['hippocampus','hippocampal-circuit','memory-consolidation','ltp','ltd','spike-timing','structural-plasticity'],'Hippocampal systems context plus supplemental cellular plasticity examples; the latter are not complete accounts of remembering.'),
  chapter('108',12,'The speaking brain',[299,330],
    'Separate recognizing spoken words, accessing meaning, combining words into sentences and retrieving speech output. Language emerges from connected systems rather than two isolated boxes. Tasks and error patterns help identify which computation is disrupted.',
    [['Input, meaning and output','Acoustic/phonological analysis, semantic knowledge and speech production have distinguishable demands that interact during normal language use.'],['Distributed language pathways','Connections link temporal and frontal territories; pathway or network damage can affect different operations. Simple historical labels are an incomplete map.'],['Sentence processing','Syntax, meaning and context jointly constrain comprehension and production. A difficult sentence contrast can also recruit memory and executive resources.']],
    ['All aphasia is just a problem in Broca’s area or Wernicke’s area.','Language difficulties can arise from several regions and connections, affecting different components. Assess actual tasks and errors instead of assuming one syndrome has one precise location.'],
    [['Someone can repeat a word but cannot explain its meaning. What distinction is useful?','Phonological input/output processing can be distinguished from semantic access. Test additional comprehension and production tasks before inferring a specific neural mechanism.'],['Why match sentence conditions for memory demand?','A neural or behavioral difference might otherwise reflect maintaining and manipulating information rather than sentence structure itself.']],['association-fibers','cerebral-cortex'],'Anatomical scaffolding only; no complete language-network or aphasia 3D model currently exists.'),
  chapter('108',13,'The literate brain',[331,358],
    'Reading links a learned visual code with language representations. Contrast familiar and unfamiliar words, regular and irregular spelling, and reading with writing to distinguish proposed computations. Orthographies and experience influence the demands.',
    [['Orthography to language','Visual word processing must connect letter patterns with pronunciation and meaning. The same physical letters can require different lexical and phonological operations.'],['Reading routes and evidence','Word/nonword and regularity manipulations test lexical and spelling-to-sound accounts. Error patterns constrain models without making a diagram a literal neural map.'],['Writing is related but distinct','Spelling, letter-form production and handwriting involve additional demands. Reading and spelling need not be identically impaired after damage.']],
    ['Reading is an innate visual faculty that works identically in every script.','Literacy is learned and recruits existing visual/language systems. Script, instruction and experience influence how written forms connect with sound and meaning.'],
    [['Why compare irregular words with pronounceable nonwords?','They place different demands on lexical knowledge and spelling-to-sound generalization, helping distinguish reading accounts. Frequency and other confounds need control.'],['How could preserved reading with impaired spelling inform a model?','It suggests at least partially distinguishable resources or pathways for the two tasks. Confirm the pattern across matched materials and rule out motor-output explanations.']],[],'This chapter has original notes and retrieval questions; the current50-topic library has no dedicated reading/writing model.'),
  chapter('108',14,'The numerate brain',[359,384],
    'Distinguish judging quantity from identifying number symbols, retrieving arithmetic facts and carrying out procedures. Behavioral effects and neurological dissociations help test how numerical knowledge is represented and accessed.',
    [['Quantity and symbols','Nonsymbolic magnitude and learned symbolic notation are related but not identical. Compare task demands before equating their representations.'],['Numerical effects','Distance, size and notation effects constrain magnitude-processing accounts. Accuracy and response time can reveal different aspects of performance.'],['Multiple operations','Reading a number, comparing quantities and retrieving an arithmetic fact can dissociate. Models specify transformations among representations rather than one universal number center.']],
    ['Good arithmetic means every numerical process is intact.','Arithmetic performance combines several skills. Number recognition, magnitude comparison, fact retrieval and procedural calculation should be tested separately.'],
    [['Why might close quantities take longer to compare than distant quantities?','Their representations or evidence are harder to distinguish. The distance effect constrains a magnitude-processing account but does not alone establish one neural code.'],['How can an arithmetic deficit differ from a general language deficit?','Compare numerical operations with matched language and control tasks. A selective impairment can reveal distinguishable resources, while task difficulty and strategy still matter.']],[],'No numerical-cognition 3D scene exists in the current library; these study prompts cover the chapter’s conceptual distinctions.'),
  chapter('108',15,'The executive brain',[385,414],
    'Executive tasks involve maintaining goals, selecting rules, updating information and resolving competition. These demands overlap but can be distinguished experimentally. Explain a task’s control requirements before attaching an executive label to a brain area.',
    [['Task analysis','A task such as switching or interference control also requires perception, memory and responses. Contrast conditions to isolate the proposed control contribution.'],['Prefrontal organization','Lateral, medial and orbital prefrontal territories interact with other systems. Several architectures can explain apparent specialization.'],['Monitoring and adjustment','Conflict, error, effort and value can influence control-related responses. An anterior cingulate signal needs an explicit interpretation and comparison.']],
    ['The frontal lobe is a single executive that commands the rest of the brain.','Control emerges through distributed interactions. Different tasks and subregions make distinguishable contributions without requiring a miniature central decision maker.'],
    [['What else can explain a task-switching cost besides switching itself?','Rule retrieval, preparation, stimulus ambiguity, response selection and carryover interference can contribute. Designs and timing manipulations help separate them.'],['Why is reverse inference especially risky for an executive activation?','Many tasks share control, effort and monitoring demands. Observing an area that activates in numerous settings is weak evidence for one uniquely identified process.']],['cerebral-cortex','basal-ganglia','thalamus','association-fibers'],'Supporting frontostriatal and cortical anatomy; executive tasks themselves are explained in the notes.'),
  chapter('108',16,'The social and emotional brain',[415,450],
    'Distinguish emotion, bodily responses, recognizing expressions and representing another person’s beliefs. Social and emotional cognition involve interacting systems and context. Compare psychological theories with physiological, lesion and imaging evidence.',
    [['Emotion components','Feeling, appraisal, expression and bodily change can be measured separately. Theories differ in how they connect those components.'],['Distributed affective systems','Amygdala and hypothalamic functions are part of wider networks. Responses can reflect relevance, learning or bodily regulation, not one universally specific emotion.'],['Other minds and social signals','Recognizing a face or expression differs from reasoning about another person’s belief. Tasks need controls for language, memory and general difficulty.']],
    ['Amygdala activation uniquely identifies fear, or facial expressions reveal thoughts with certainty.','Amygdala responses are not fear-exclusive, and expression interpretation depends on context. Neither a single activation nor an expression is a direct readout of an internal state.'],
    [['How would you separate belief reasoning from general story comprehension?','Compare stories or tasks matched for language and memory but differing in demands to represent a person’s belief, then test alternative control explanations.'],['Why measure physiology and subjective reports separately in an emotion study?','They are related but distinct components and need not change in lockstep. Their pattern helps distinguish theories without treating one measure as the entire emotion.']],['amygdala','hypothalamus','cerebral-cortex'],'Deep-region and bodily-regulation context only; no model identifies a person’s emotion or belief from shape.')
];

const reisberg=[
  chapter('105',1,'The Science of the Mind',[2,23],
    'Cognitive psychology explains how information is acquired, represented and used. Mental operations are inferred from observable performance using competing predictions. Background knowledge shapes everyday understanding, even when a task does not explicitly ask you to remember.',
    [['Inference from behavior','Accuracy, error patterns and response times constrain accounts of hidden processes. A useful theory predicts more than a label for behavior.'],['Limits of introspection','Reports describe experience but cannot reliably expose every unconscious operation. Independent measurements help test explanations.'],['Cognitive revolution','Representations and information processing allowed hypotheses about the mind to be tested alongside behavior rather than excluded from scientific explanation.']],
    ['A process is unscientific because it cannot be directly seen.','Invisible mechanisms can be tested through observable predictions. The important distinction is between testable accounts and explanations that cannot be challenged by evidence.'],
    [['Two theories explain the same accuracy. What other measurement might distinguish them?','Response times, characteristic errors or transfer to a new condition can reveal different predicted operations. The choice should follow the theories’ explicit predictions.'],['Why can understanding a short story depend on memory without an explicit memory test?','Prior knowledge supplies meanings, inferences and expectations. Those representations help interpret the new information.']],[]),
  chapter('105',2,'The Neural Basis for Cognition',[24,60],
    'Relate cognitive functions to brain organization without turning lobes into isolated mental faculties. Neurons and glia form interacting systems. Lesion and imaging evidence illuminate specialization, while connected processing is necessary for most everyday tasks.',
    [['Anatomy and connectivity','Learn lobes, deep structures and commissural connections in a stated view. Specialized regions interact through pathways rather than working in isolation.'],['Evidence about function','Brain damage and task-related activity answer different questions. Combine them with behavioral experiments to constrain causal and representational claims.'],['Neurons and synapses','Electrical signals propagate within neurons; chemical transmission usually links neurons. Receptor and gradient context determine a synaptic response.']],
    ['A cognitive ability resides entirely inside one labeled lobe.','Most tasks depend on several connected operations. A lesion or activation pattern can indicate a contribution without identifying the whole ability with one location.'],
    [['Why can severing a connection disrupt behavior without destroying both connected regions?','A system depends on communication as well as its component regions. Disconnection can prevent information from being combined or used by another part of the network.'],['How does a synapse differ from the axon that reaches it?','An axon conducts a regenerated electrical event. At a chemical synapse, transmitter release changes receptor conductances across a small extracellular gap.']],['brain-overview','cerebral-cortex','thalamus','hypothalamus','hippocampus','amygdala','cerebellum','brainstem','corpus-callosum','white-matter','association-fibers','neuron','dendrites','myelin','resting-potential','synaptic-release'],'Introductory anatomical foundations; model fine details extend beyond the chapter.'),
  chapter('105',3,'Visual Perception',[62,101],
    'Perception actively organizes and interprets visual input. Receptive fields and parallel pathways explain some feature processing, while grouping, depth cues and constancy show why the retinal pattern alone does not determine experience.',
    [['Coding and parallel processing','Visual pathways emphasize different features. Distributed processing creates the problem of linking the features of the same object.'],['Organization','Grouping principles and context help organize edges into objects. An interpretation can influence which features are perceived.'],['Constancy and depth','Size, shape and distance judgments use multiple cues. An ordinarily useful interpretation can produce a predictable illusion.']],
    ['Perception is a pixel-for-pixel copy of the retinal image.','The same physical input can support different interpretations. Perception combines input with organization and contextual assumptions to produce useful estimates.'],
    [['Why can a smaller retinal image be perceived as the same-sized object farther away?','Perceived distance helps interpret retinal size. Constancy depends on jointly using several cues rather than reading retinal size alone.'],['Why is the binding problem a consequence of parallel processing?','Different features are analyzed in different systems. The system must combine the appropriate features into coherent object representations.']],['optic-radiation','thalamus','cerebral-cortex'],'Visual-pathway anatomy supports the chapter; grouping and perceptual experiments are covered in the notes.'),
  chapter('105',4,'Recognizing Objects',[102,141],
    'Object recognition is influenced by features, familiarity, recent experience and context. Word and face tasks reveal different constraints on recognition accounts. Evaluate what a model predicts for ambiguous inputs and systematic errors.',
    [['Feature and detector accounts','A recognition model links lower-level evidence to candidate patterns. Thresholds, competition and feedback can generate specific recognition predictions.'],['Word recognition effects','Frequency, repetition priming and the word-superiority effect constrain explanations of how context aids identifying a printed pattern.'],['Faces and configuration','Feature identity and relationships among features can contribute differently. A face-recognition account must explain configuration effects as well as local parts.']],
    ['Top-down influence makes recognition arbitrary or independent of the input.','Context interacts with incoming evidence. It often improves efficiency but can bias an ambiguous input toward a familiar interpretation.'],
    [['Why is recognizing a letter inside a word a useful comparison with an isolated letter?','It tests how context and interactions among detectors affect identifying the same kind of visual component. Presentation and task conditions must be matched.'],['How can a successful recognition model also predict errors?','An efficient system uses regularities and expectations to resolve incomplete input. Those same mechanisms can produce systematic misidentifications under ambiguous conditions.']],[]),
  chapter('105',5,'Paying Attention',[142,184],
    'Attention selects information and allocates limited processing resources. Selection can follow space, objects or goals, and dividing attention depends on task demands and practice. Distinguish an unnoticed stimulus from a stimulus that was never physically available.',
    [['Selection','Dichotic listening and inattentional-blindness tasks test what is processed or reported when attention is committed elsewhere.'],['Resources and preparation','Anticipating a location or stimulus can improve processing while competing demands reduce available resources. Some costs are task-specific and others more general.'],['Practice and automaticity','Practice can reduce effort or decision demands. Automatic processing can improve efficiency while becoming harder to interrupt or control.']],
    ['Multitasking is cost-free once tasks feel familiar.','Practice can reduce demands but does not remove every conflict, bottleneck or switching cost. Performance must be measured rather than inferred from confidence.'],
    [['What would count as evidence for object-based rather than purely space-based selection?','Performance differs when attention shifts within versus between objects despite comparable spatial distances, under conditions that rule out simple location differences.'],['Why does missing an unexpected object not prove the visual system received no input?','The stimulus can reach sensory systems without being selected, consciously reported or remembered. Those are distinct processing outcomes.']],[]),
  chapter('105',6,'The Acquisition of Memories and the Working-Memory System',[186,225],
    'Learning depends on how information is processed and connected with existing knowledge. Working memory supports ongoing storage and manipulation, while elaboration creates useful retrieval routes. Study behavior should prepare you for the kind of remembering you will need.',
    [['Working memory','Active information is maintained and manipulated through interacting resources. Capacity depends on task demands, knowledge and organization, not a universal item count.'],['Elaboration and meaning','Explaining relationships and connecting ideas can create effective retrieval cues. Mere repetition can maintain availability without the same long-term benefit.'],['Learning and retrieval','Acquisition, storage and retrieval are useful distinctions but interact. Understanding and organization influence later access.']],
    ['Wanting to memorize or repeatedly rereading guarantees learning.','The processing and retrieval demands matter. Fluency during rereading can be mistaken for the ability to explain or retrieve later.'],
    [['How would you turn passive reading into elaborative learning?','Explain a mechanism in your own words, connect it to a contrasting example and retrieve it without looking. Then use feedback to correct gaps.'],['Why can chunking improve performance without simply enlarging a storage box?','Knowledge organizes several elements into meaningful units and reduces processing demands. The benefit depends on what the learner already knows.']],['memory-consolidation','hippocampus'],'Models provide biological background; they do not depict the behavioral working-memory system.'),
  chapter('105',7,'The Many Types of Memory',[226,263],
    'Memory performance depends on cues, context and the type of test. Recall, recognition, familiarity, source memory and implicit effects can diverge. Ask what information the test requires rather than labeling a person’s memory globally good or poor.',
    [['Encoding specificity','Learning binds information with aspects of its context. A cue helps when it gives access to relevant traces or connections.'],['Implicit and explicit tests','Prior experience can influence performance without conscious recollection. Different tests may reveal learning that another test misses.'],['Amnesia and interpretation','A failure of episodic recall can coexist with preserved priming or skill improvement. Test demands distinguish these capacities.']],
    ['Failure to consciously recall means nothing was learned.','Implicit performance effects can reveal prior learning without conscious remembering. Demonstrate the effect with an appropriate comparison rather than assuming it exists.'],
    [['Why can a cue that helped at study fail at test?','It may not provide access to the information or context encoded during learning. Retrieval depends on the interaction between trace and available cues.'],['How can you test learning without asking whether the learner remembers the episode?','Compare a performance measure such as identification or a practiced skill after prior exposure with a suitable unexposed baseline, while controlling alternative causes.']],['hippocampus','hippocampal-circuit','memory-consolidation'],'Memory-system foundations; current models do not simulate a behavioral priming or source-memory test.'),
  chapter('105',8,'Remembering Complex Events',[264,304],
    'Remembering an event reconstructs information using traces, connections and prior knowledge. Those mechanisms make memory useful but can also produce source errors and distortions. Separate accuracy, confidence and the conditions under which remembering is assessed.',
    [['Reconstruction and schemas','Knowledge supports understanding and retrieval but can encourage plausible details that were not part of the original event.'],['Misinformation and source','Later information can influence a report. Ask whether the learner distinguishes the original episode from a suggestion or inference.'],['Forgetting and access','Interference, cue availability and changes in traces can contribute. A failed retrieval is not a direct measurement of erased storage.']],
    ['A vivid, confident memory must be accurate.','Detail and confidence can be influenced by feedback and reconstruction. Their diagnostic value depends on the conditions; they are not guarantees of truth.'],
    [['Why can a schema both help recall and produce an error?','It supplies organization and retrieval cues, but can also add expected details or merge an inference with what actually occurred.'],['How would you test a misinformation effect?','Compare later memory reports after misleading versus appropriate control information, using matched procedures and avoiding additional suggestion in the test.']],['memory-consolidation'],'Systems-level background only; the model is not a simulation of false memory or an eyewitness account.'),
  chapter('105',9,'Concepts and Generic Knowledge',[306,341],
    'Concepts support categorization and inference without requiring a dictionary-like definition for every category. Prototypes, remembered examples and causal knowledge make different predictions. Consider which properties matter in a particular judgment and why.',
    [['Typicality','Category members can differ in how representative they seem. Typicality effects do not by themselves identify one exclusive storage format.'],['Prototypes and exemplars','A summary representation and memory for specific examples can each contribute to judgments. Compare their predictions for new cases.'],['Knowledge and relations','Causal beliefs and relationships among concepts shape what counts as relevant similarity. Surface resemblance is not the whole account.']],
    ['Being atypical means an item is not a genuine category member.','Typicality and membership can differ. An unusual example can clearly belong to a category even if it shares few familiar surface features.'],
    [['What prediction could distinguish a prototype from an exemplar account?','Test judgments for new items whose similarity to an average pattern differs from their similarity to specific learned examples, while controlling overall familiarity.'],['Why can knowing an object’s history change its category without changing its appearance?','Conceptual judgments can use causal or essential-property beliefs rather than visual resemblance alone.']],[]),
  chapter('105',10,'Language',[342,383],
    'Language combines structured sound patterns, words and sentences with meaning and context. Comprehension is incremental, so an early interpretation can need revision. Distinguish linguistic knowledge from prescribed rules about how people ought to speak.',
    [['Structure across levels','Phonemes, morphemes and syntactic patterns serve different roles. Their combinations are constrained by learned and biological capacities.'],['Parsing in context','Listeners and readers interpret incoming words as they arrive. Syntax, semantics and context jointly influence the developing interpretation.'],['Pragmatics and learning','Communication uses common ground and inference. Language acquisition and aphasia provide evidence about capacities and component processes.']],
    ['Grammar is only a list of school rules, or language determines every thought one can have.','Linguistic rules describe systematic knowledge used in comprehension/production. Language can guide attention and formulation without proving all possible thought is limited to words.'],
    [['What does a garden-path sentence reveal about comprehension?','An initially plausible parse can become incompatible with later words and require revision. This supports incremental processing while leaving several accounts of its constraints possible.'],['Why is a phoneme different from a morpheme?','A phoneme is a contrastive sound unit; a morpheme is a meaning-bearing grammatical unit. One describes sound organization, the other how form contributes to meaning.']],[]),
  chapter('105',11,'Visual Knowledge',[384,426],
    'Mental imagery can preserve spatial and visual relationships without being a literal internal photograph. Response-time tasks, interference, neural evidence and individual differences help study imagery beyond self-report. Distinguish visual detail from spatial operations.',
    [['Chronometric evidence','Mental rotation or scanning times vary with task demands and imagined relationships. The task must rule out simpler response strategies and expectation effects.'],['Imagery and perception','They can share mechanisms and interfere with or facilitate each other, but shared activity does not imply identical experience or processing.'],['Representation and knowledge','Images are interpreted through a reference frame and prior knowledge. Verbal, visual and spatial formats can contribute differently to memory.']],
    ['A vivid image is a complete photograph stored in the mind.','Imagery can be selective, interpreted and reconstructed. Vividness does not guarantee complete detail, accuracy or the same spatial abilities in every task.'],
    [['How can response time test a claim about mental rotation?','Vary the required angular transformation while controlling stimulus/response differences, then compare the observed pattern with predictions of alternative strategies.'],['Why should visual imagery and spatial ability be measured separately?','They can rely on partly different processes and can dissociate across people or tasks. One self-report score need not capture every relevant ability.']],[]),
  chapter('105',12,'Judgment and Reasoning',[428,471],
    'Judgments can use efficient shortcuts or more effortful analysis, with both strategy and context affecting performance. Evaluate evidence, sample size, base rates and the framing of choices. A plausible conclusion is not the same thing as a valid argument.',
    [['Heuristics','Availability and representativeness can be useful but can misweight salience, sample size or base rates. Compare the shortcut with the actual question.'],['Evidence and logic','Confirmation and belief biases influence what evidence is sought and how arguments are evaluated. Look for tests that could disconfirm a claim.'],['Decisions in context','Descriptions of gains/losses, justification and emotion can alter choices. A decision model must explain these patterns rather than assume perfect utility calculation.']],
    ['A fast judgment is always wrong and a slow judgment is always rational.','Experience and task structure can support good intuition, while effortful thinking can still be biased. Evaluate the evidence and strategy, not speed alone.'],
    [['Why can an accurate diagnostic test still yield many false positive results for a rare event?','The base rate affects how many tested cases truly have the event. Reason using natural-frequency counts of true and false results rather than test accuracy alone.'],['How does looking for disconfirming evidence improve a test of a belief?','It asks what would challenge the proposed explanation and reduces reliance on a selectively supportive sample. A competing account can help identify informative cases.']],[]),
  chapter('105',13,'Problem Solving and Creativity',[472,503],
    'Solving a problem depends on representing it, searching possible actions and using relevant experience. Useful heuristics and familiar sets can become obstacles when a new representation is needed. Creative products can emerge through ordinary processes combined effectively.',
    [['Search and subgoals','Hill climbing and means–end analysis reduce an otherwise large search space, but can overlook routes that require a temporary setback.'],['Analogy and expertise','Deeper structural similarity can make a previous solution useful. Surface resemblance can distract from the relationship that transfers.'],['Representation and fixation','Functional fixedness and an established approach can hide alternatives. Reframing the goal or constraints can change the available solutions.']],
    ['An insight feels sudden, so it must be a special infallible unconscious solution.','A promising new representation still needs verification. Sudden awareness does not establish its correctness or a unique underlying mechanism.'],
    [['Why might an expert recognize a useful analogy that a novice misses?','The expert can represent the underlying relations rather than only surface objects, making an earlier solution accessible as a transferable structure.'],['How could changing a diagram solve a problem without adding new facts?','It changes which relationships and constraints are explicit, reducing misleading assumptions and opening different search paths.']],[]),
  chapter('105',14,'Intelligence',[504,533],
    'Distinguish how an ability is measured from explanations of why people differ. Reliability, validity and correlations among tasks constrain models of intelligence. Scores reflect particular tests and conditions, while development involves interacting genetic and environmental influences.',
    [['Measurement','Reliability concerns consistency; validity concerns what interpretations a measure supports. Neither makes a score a complete inventory of a person’s abilities.'],['Structure of abilities','General and more specific abilities can contribute to performance. Fluid problem solving and acquired knowledge are useful distinctions within broader models.'],['Development and environment','Population variation, opportunities, education and other conditions affect scores. Heritability is not an individual destiny or an explanation of group differences.']],
    ['A heritable test score fixes a person’s potential or explains differences between groups.','Within-population heritability estimates cannot establish those claims. Measurement conditions and environmental differences require independent evidence and causal analysis.'],
    [['How can a test be reliable yet fail a claimed interpretation?','It can consistently measure something other than the proposed construct, or support only narrower predictions. Reliability is necessary for many uses but does not establish validity alone.'],['What distinguishes fluid from crystallized intelligence?','Fluid ability concerns reasoning in relatively novel situations; crystallized ability concerns acquired knowledge and skills. Actual tasks can involve both.']],[]),
  chapter('105',15,'Conscious Thought, Unconscious Thought',[534,572],
    'Much processing occurs without awareness of its operations. Conscious reports, successful performance and flexible use of information are different observations. Theories of consciousness must explain these patterns while distinguishing access to information from subjective experience.',
    [['Products versus processes','People can experience a result without observing how it was produced. Introspection is not a full processing trace.'],['Unconscious influences','Priming, habits and some residual performance can occur without explicit awareness. Their scope needs controlled tests and does not imply an all-knowing hidden mind.'],['Access and experience','Access consciousness concerns information available for use and report; phenomenal consciousness concerns subjective experience. A theory’s evidence may bear differently on each.']],
    ['Correct performance proves conscious awareness, or unreported processing proves no information was available.','Performance, reports and flexible use can dissociate. The task and measurement determine which claim the evidence supports.'],
    [['Why compare forced-choice performance with awareness reports?','They measure different outcomes. A dissociation can reveal processing not captured by a report, while response criteria and measurement sensitivity still need examination.'],['What does a global-workspace account try to explain?','How information becomes broadly available to otherwise specialized systems for flexible use, integration and report. It is a theoretical account rather than a settled identification of subjective experience.']],['cerebral-cortex','corpus-callosum'],'Network/disconnection background only; no current 3D model represents consciousness itself.')
];

